# Pi workflow reference

## Launch and ownership (last verified 2026-09-07)

Use the managed `pi` zsh function. It runs the repository's lockfile-pinned CLI
(`node_modules/.bin/pi`) on every platform — there is no Brew or metapac
install — disables native tools and ambient extensions, then loads the managed
workflow and Herdr when present. Running a pi binary outside the function
bypasses this managed launch configuration.

The workflow code pi loads is the applied copy under `~/.pi/agent/workflow`,
not the repository source; `~/.pi/agent/node_modules` is a symlink to the
repository's pinned `node_modules`, which pi's documented "node_modules in a
parent directory" lookup follows. A source edit reaches a running or future
session only through `chezmoi apply`, the same gate as Claude's hooks or
Codex's config.

The repository's npm manifest/lockfile is the single source for the CLI, the
extension dependencies, and the worker SDK — one pinned unit (currently
{{ index (fromJson (include "package.json")).dependencies "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent" }}),
so CLI/SDK skew cannot occur. Pi breaks extension APIs across 0.x point
releases: upgrade by bumping that one pin, then `npm ci`, `npm run test:pi`,
and `chezmoi apply`. The npm dependency hook installs with lifecycle scripts
disabled.

Settings are merged by managed key; unrelated UI/trust preferences survive.
Authentication and session files remain unmanaged. Sign in through Pi's normal
OpenAI subscription login; do not import another harness's auth files.

