## Planning and permissions (last verified 2026-09-07)

- New sessions start in plan mode with auto-reviewed approvals. Source writes
  remain disabled; temporary build/cache artifacts use designated writable roots.
- `/execute` or approval of `submit_plan` explicitly enables scoped execution.
  `/plan` revokes child sessions and sandbox processes before enabling planning.
- `/approvals` selects auto review or individual prompts. Hard denial, inherited
  role scope and mode restrictions run before approval; approval cannot widen them.
- Shell commands run inside the SRT profile without review, as sandboxed
  commands do in Claude Code and Codex; the profile is the boundary. Only
  remote-mutating verbs the profile cannot judge — `git push`, `gh` other than
  view/list/status/search/diff/checks, `gh api` with a method or field,
  `npm publish`, `docker push`, `curl`/`wget` with data, json or upload flags,
  `ssh`, `scp`, `sftp`, `rsync` — go to the auto classifier, then to a confirm
  dialog on `ask`. File edits inside the workspace run without review once
  execution is approved.
- Classifier errors/uncertainty prompt the root session; unattended requests deny.
  A pending dialog is announced in the footer and times out after ten minutes.
- `ask_user_question` (the pinned `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` plugin)
  presents up to four questions with options, previews and free text in one
  tabbed dialog; answers land in the transcript and the classifier's task
  context. Approval dialogs are once-only; MCP approvals are never cached
  across a mode change.
- `workspace_bash` with `run_in_background: true` starts a background task
  (Claude Code's flag): the call returns a task id at once, the worker keeps
  its lease, and when the command ends the model hears a steer message with
  the exit status and the last twenty lines (with its next tool result, or
  as a new turn when idle). `workspace_task` `{ id, action: "output" | "stop" }`
  reads the output so far or stops it. Output is kept in memory, tail-capped
  at 256 KiB. `/plan` and `/remove-dir` stop running tasks like any sandbox
  process (they end as `stopped`); so does exiting pi.
- `/add-dir <path>` (or `/add-dir` for a prompt) adds a directory to the
  editable workspace, as Claude Code's does: edits inside it are allowed once
  execution is approved, its `.git`, `.pi` and every relative deny entry
  (`.env`) are protected there as under cwd, and its `AGENTS.md` (or
  `CLAUDE.md`) is appended to the system prompt after passing the read policy
  (a symlink out of the directory or onto a denied path refuses the add).
  Home, its ancestors, denied paths and directories already in scope are
  refused with the reason. `/remove-dir` picks one to drop. State is per session; skills
  under an added directory need a restart with `--skill <path>`, since pi
  discovers resources only at startup and `/reload`. Reads were never
  confined to the workspace; only the deny list applies.
- If a process disappears without acknowledging termination, the workflow blocks.
  Check orphan processes and restart; do not treat a lost connection as proof that
  a writer stopped.

The `workspace_*` tools are the SDK's own tools running in the host Pi process;
each invocation leases a per-tool SRT worker that performs the actual file and
shell operations, so effects and subprocess execution are sandboxed — not all
file I/O (path probes, diff generation, and output temp files stay host-side).
Every lease consumes a single-use ticket minted by its approval, so a mode
change between approval and execution invalidates the lease. Interactive `!`
commands use the same worker boundary. MCP stdio servers also run inside SRT,
while OAuth stays in the trusted host Pi process.

Sensitive paths come from the shared denylist. File tools additionally resolve
symlinks and confine edits to the workspace; git metadata and live-loaded code
(`~/.pi/agent`, the repository `node_modules`, `~/.zshrc`) are write-protected
in every mode, while the repository source is editable once execution is
approved. A refusal names its reason (`Writes are disabled in this scope`,
`Path denied by managed policy`, `File edits must remain inside the
workspace`); the model should report it rather than re-submit the plan. This is
an explicit denylist, not a guarantee against unknown credential locations. Add
new credential paths to shared policy before exposing them to a session.

Extensions are trusted host code. Per-tool SRT is not containment against a
malicious extension. No fallback to unsandboxed execution exists in the managed
tools. Linux needs bubblewrap, socat and ripgrep; macOS needs sandbox-exec.

## Specialists, skills and display (last verified 2026-09-08, dot form; unapplied)

Specialists under `~/.pi/agent/agents` render from the shared roster. Each has an
explicit OpenAI model, tool list and mandatory child policy extension. Native
builtin names cannot substitute for missing `workspace_*` tools. Read-only
specialists stay read-only even when the root enters execution. Specialists are
leaf roles; only `general-purpose` (every tool, the session's model) may launch
children, to the same depth as Claude Code's built-in general-purpose agent.

Use named `subagent` launches with an agent and bounded task; the roster is in
the tool description, so no `list` call is needed. Every child runs in the
background and up to twenty share the root policy (Claude Code's default), so
batch the independent launches that each repay their handoff into one turn. Children start fresh; `context:
"fork"` branches this session's transcript into the child, as Codex's forked
children do, for a second pass over material already read. Unsupported launch
keys the upstream schema still advertises (`cwd`, `toolBudget`, …) are dropped
silently; workflow scripts, task lists and chains are refused. Status (fleet or
transcript view), stop, interrupt and non-recovering steer are enabled;
project role overrides, revival and mutating management are not. Results
arrive as notifications — `bg_wait` only when the current turn depends on them.

Display follows `docs/pi-design.md` in the repository. Reasoning never shows;
a tool row is `• title` plus one `↳` summary line (`+12 −4`, `4 matches`,
`31 lines · ctrl+o to expand`, `no output`), MCP calls included (`• exa ›
web_search_exa "query"`), a subagent launch is `• agent › task` with
`↳ launched`, and any other plugin tool (`bg_wait`, the questionnaire's
answers entry) takes the same row; a failed shell command shows its first two
and last two lines with `… N more lines` between. The `workspace_*` and MCP
rows between two things that stay visible fold to one dim line above them
("Read 3 files, ran 9 shell commands, called 2 MCP tools"); clicking that
line opens or closes the group, Ctrl+O opens it. Whatever stays visible
closes the group — assistant text, a subagent or other plugin row, a failed
row (its group's last), a completion line, the turn line, the next run — and
the next foldable row starts a new one. An async child
that ends prints `• agent finished · task · 2m 14s`, or its status word
(`failed`/`stopped` red, `paused`/`partial`/`detached` amber). Reasoning
never renders (the markdown transformer empties `assistant-thinking`;
`hideThinkingBlock` stays off), though pi's assistant component still adds
its one spacer line per message that carried reasoning, and one more between
reasoning and text — the component reads the raw content, and no documented
API reaches it (earendil-works/pi#8154); with every tool row's own leading
blank that makes two blank lines wherever the model reasoned between tool
calls. Lines that open with a glyph (`•`, `π`, `❯`) start at column 0, lines
without one (`↳`, the fold line, the status line, the fleet rows) two columns
in; pi's `outputPad` is 0 so its own lines match, and assistant text
therefore sits at column 0 under its bullet rather than at Claude Code's
text column (`outputPad` is 0/1 by design, pi#6168/#6757; no assistant
renderer hook, pi#6747/#6876 — re-check if any of these closes). A reply
that opens with a heading renders it bold on the bullet line.
While a turn runs the working spinner reads
`Interpolating… 1m 12s` (a random π-flavoured verb from `rows.mjs`) in the
composer's top shaded row; when it
ends a `π Interpolated for 3m 34s · done 9:04 pm` line is appended — only once
no background child is live, otherwise after the child's follow-up run settles
(or the user types); ctrl+c prints `π Interrupted after …`. The status line
carries model · context · usage · branch on the left and the workflow mode on
the right.

Running children hang under the status line as `○ agent › title · tokens ·
model` rows (title cut at a word boundary to 36 columns; five rows, then
`↓ N more`; queued children dim). The composer is the user box's shape: a
block in `userMessageBg` with a blank shaded row above (the spinner while a
turn runs) and below, a `❯` prompt at column 0, no rule lines, no
placeholder; those two are the only backgrounds. Arrow-down from the last line of the
prompt moves a `›` cursor into the rows; up/down move it, Enter shows the
highlighted child's transcript tail and model in an overlay (Esc closes; a
child with no transcript yet says so), Esc or up past the first row returns
to the prompt, any other key returns and types. `Ctrl+Alt+F` (⌃⌥F on macOS)
opens pi-subagents' inspector (steer, stop, full transcripts). On exit pi
restores the screen and prints only the resume command
(`fullscreenExitOutput: resume-hint`).

Coupling inventory (re-check on every pin bump; `npm run test:pi` is the gate):

- Documented pi surfaces: tool renderers (`renderShell: "self"`,
  `context.expanded/toolCallId/invalidate/state`), `registerMarkdownTransformer`,
  `appendEntry`/`registerEntryRenderer`, `setFooter`, `setHeader`,
  `setEditorComponent`, `setWorkingMessage`, the `outputPad` and
  `hideThinkingBlock` settings, the `agent_start/agent_end/agent_settled`,
  `message_update/message_end`, `tool_execution_*`, `ui_prompt_*` and `input`
  events; pi-subagents' `subagents:rpc:v1` status reply and
  `subagent:async-started/complete` events. `stability.test.mjs` checks every
  import and event name against the package exports and docs.
- Plugin rows: pi-subagents, pi-mcp-adapter, the questionnaire, todo and
  web-search plugins receive a Proxy of the extension API whose
  `registerTool` swaps `renderShell`/`renderCall`/`renderResult` on every
  registration (`index.mjs` `pluginApi`; `subagent`, `bg_wait`, the
  supervisor channel, `mcp`, `mcpScript`, `mcp__*`, `ask_user_question`,
  `todo`, `web_search`, `url_context`); rests on the plugins
  registering through the API they are handed and pi keeping the definition
  object (`loader.js`). Rows read `args` and the result text; the two
  `details` reads are the adapter's `error` (failures it reports without
  `isError`) and pi-subagents' `asyncId` (a launch). No upstream override API
  exists (earendil-works/pi#3541); `stability.test.mjs` pins the
  registrations and both fields.
- Session surfaces: pi's `resetExtensionUI` (on `/new`, `/resume`) clears the
  header, footer and custom editor, so `session_start` re-applies them every
  time; pinned in `stability.test.mjs`.
- Exported but undocumented: `renderDiff`, `keyHint`, `getMarkdownTheme`,
  `CustomEditor` (stability test covers the export; `caret.test.mjs` pins the
  render shape the prompt relies on — `renderTopBorder`/`renderBottomBorder`,
  `setPaddingX`, the first content line's padding columns). The spinner in
  the top row rests on the documented `embedWorkingStatus` option plus two
  undocumented pieces: pi handing a `WorkingStatusIndicator` to the editor's
  `setWorkingStatusIndicator` (and only that kind), and that indicator's
  `renderInBorder(width)`; both pinned in `stability.test.mjs`.
- Mouse: the fold handle answers clicks on its summary line from its own
  `handleMouse`; rests on pi-tui's `MouseRegion` asking the child before its
  own handler and on `ToolExecutionComponent` forwarding self-shell mouse
  events one row up (`y - 1`). Reasoning stays out because pi-tui's `Markdown`
  renders no line when the transformer returns "" (an empty hidden-thinking
  label is not empty: pi wraps it in colour codes, which rendered an
  invisible clickable line). Pinned in `stability.test.mjs`.
- Heuristic: fleet rows pair with async runs by agent label, a 30 s start
  window and sibling rank (`fleet.mjs` `runIdFor`); the DTO's keys are opaque
  and its `goal` is never filled in 0.66.0. `fleet.test.mjs` pins it; the
  upstream fix is a `goal`-populating DTO. Completion lines take each result's
  resolved `status` from the `subagent:async-complete` payload (the result
  file spread plus `runId`), falling back to `state`, `success` and `agent`,
  the duration from the same file's `durationMs` (launch to end), and the task
  from the launch's own tool events, since `async-started` redacts it. Row
  text assumes the bash tool's `(no output)` stand-in and `Command exited
  with code N` trailer, and pi's per-row click toggling one row's `expanded`
  (a body only; a closed fold reopens when the documented
  `ctx.ui.getToolsExpanded()` value changes, i.e. on Ctrl+O). All pinned in
  `stability.test.mjs`.
- Plugin data the rows and policy read: the todo tool's `details.tasks`,
  `details.action` and `details.error`; pi-web-search's `details.error` and
  its `web_search`/`url_context` names; the SDK bash schema being a plain
  object whose `properties` take the `run_in_background` flag. All pinned in
  `stability.test.mjs`, with `pi.sendMessage`, `ctx.ui.input` and
  `ctx.ui.select` checked against the docs.
- Outside the npm pin: the status line's usage segments come from `codex
  app-server` JSON-RPC (`account/rateLimits/read`), a codex-cli surface with no
  published contract: re-verify after every codex-cli bump (last verified
  against 0.153.4); a failed read only drops the segments.

Shared skills remain discoverable under `~/.agents/skills`; explicit skill loading
is available through Pi. Inside a child, pi-subagents'
tool allowlist may leave the direct `mcp__*` tools inactive; the `mcp` proxy is
always in the allowlist and reaches the same servers.

## Task list and web search (last verified 2026-09-08, evening; unapplied)

`todo` is the pinned `@juicesharp/rpiv-todo` plugin (Claude Code's task
list): `create`/`update`/`list`/`get`/`delete`/`clear`, a panel above the
composer that survives `/reload` and compaction, `/todos` to print it,
`ctrl+shift+t` to collapse it. Root only; children carry no list. Its rows
take the transcript shape (`docs/pi-design.md` rule 10).

`web_search` is the pinned `pi-web-search` plugin: OpenAI's server-side
`web_search` tool, requested against the same Codex Responses endpoint and
with the same subscription token as model calls, so it adds no endpoint,
credential or network path beyond the provider's own — and sits in the same
terms grey zone as the provider itself. Each search is one extra model
request on the account's usage windows; `~/.pi/agent/web-search.json` (rendered
from `agents.pi.search_tier`) pins it to the small tier so the default
model's quota is untouched. Roles with Claude's `WebSearch` get it; the
plugin's Gemini-only `url_context` also registers and is in no role's list.
Fetching stays with the Exa MCP tools and Playwright (sandboxed); OpenAI's
search accepts extra `urls` to analyse alongside the query.

## MCP and code intelligence (last verified 2026-09-07, third smoke run)

Context7, Exa and Serena register their tools directly, named
`mcp__<server>_<tool>` (for example `mcp__exa_web_search_exa`), so they are
called like any other tool. Playwright stays behind the `mcp` proxy:
`mcp({ search: "…" })` finds a tool in the cached catalog,
`mcp({ describe: "<name>" })` shows its schema, `mcp({ tool: "<name>", args:
{ … } })` calls it. A server shown as not connected connects on the first
call. MCP servers run as sandboxed Node processes with `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`
so their fetch calls follow the sandbox proxy; if every call from a server
fails with `fetch failed`, check that first, then the allowed domains.
Configuration is explicit; other harnesses' private configuration is not imported.
MCP scripting, sampling and automatic OAuth discovery are disabled. The approval
broker checks the resolved server/tool/arguments, not just the outer proxy call.

There is no LSP integration (dropped 2026-09-06: per-invocation server starts
paid LSP's cost without its persistent-index benefit, and the only candidate
package was young, solo-maintained, and unproven for agents). Diagnostics come
from project toolchains via `workspace_bash` (`tsc --noEmit`, `go vet`,
pyright, linters). Serena provides symbol navigation/references and
execute-mode rename; its configuration and project caches are redirected into
session scratch. Revisit LSP only on measured pain, as a sandboxed MCP server
or a persistent broker-owned worker.

Claude/Codex proprietary plugins, cloud services and exact UI interactions are
not supplied by these packages. Keep the other harnesses for those capabilities.

## Acceptance checks (last verified 2026-09-06)

From the source repository, run `npm run test:pi`. It covers policy, classifier
fallback, terminal proof, child preflight and secret-stubbed chezmoi projections.
`PI_WORKFLOW_LIVE_TESTS=1 npm run test:pi` additionally exercises actual Unix
sockets and SRT against disposable fixtures; it needs an unrestricted local host.

Before relying on the setup, separately verify subscription login, parent and
foreground/background child model pins, auto approvals, cancellation, the fleet widget,
MCP queries, diagnostics and approved edits in a disposable project. Fixture
tests do not establish account access or live terminal behavior. Compare Sol and
Astra on matched completed tasks before changing the default model.
